WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.730
(crowd cheering)

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:03.940
Thanks for being here.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:04.820
Great to be here.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.590
You know, I, first of all I wanna say,

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:07.500
I saw this movie,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.100
I'm gonna say it's like
a week and a half ago

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.540
they got me a special screening.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.370
And I went with a couple
of the priests on our show

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.030
and we were completely
blown away by this film.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.140
It's a tall order.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.010
I would say, playing Johnny
Cash and you just become him.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:23.080
And you forget that you're
an actor playing Johnny Cash

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.220
which is the biggest hurdle in a biopic.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.176
So congratulations,
really an incredible job.

00:00:27.176 --> 00:00:28.009
Thank you.

00:00:28.009 --> 00:00:28.861
You gotta see this movie

00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:31.642
(crowd applauds)

00:00:31.642 --> 00:00:33.457
First of all I know...

00:00:33.457 --> 00:00:35.240
You know you've come on
the show a couple of times

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.420
over the years and I know

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.700
that one of the hardest things
I know that you love acting,

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.130
you love inhabiting the
role you love the craft.

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:44.050
Then you've got to go out
and talk about the movie

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:46.450
which can probably get
a little boring for you.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.720
Cause people ask you the same
questions over and over again.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.730
Is there anything you're doing
this time around to sort of,

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:54.137
keep yourself entertained as
you go through this process?

00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:56.557
(speaks foreign language)

00:00:56.557 --> 00:00:57.787
(crowd laughs)

00:00:57.787 --> 00:00:58.980
My question exactly.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.647
Yeah. No, I always, I have...

00:01:02.647 --> 00:01:04.530
(crowd laughing)

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.345
That was for our Finnish audience I think.

00:01:07.345 --> 00:01:09.260
Yeah, no, I have

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:13.380
I just create a special languages
that no one understands.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.440
I don't even understand it.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.240
Right right. You just, you,
you make up like kind of words

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.820
and stuff just to do every now and then.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.770
You've actually been doing
this on this press tour.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:21.650
Is that right?

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.176
Yeah a little bit mew mew

00:01:23.176 --> 00:01:26.445
(crowd laughing)

00:01:26.445 --> 00:01:27.310
(crosstalk)

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.000
You're doing it for yourself, right?

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Well, me and two other people.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:31.833
Right.

00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:33.950
You know, I have a friend Michael

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:35.530
and we call him "Mew mew."

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.770
And he actually has a song

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.520
that we've made up just for him.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:41.353
Just for him.

00:01:41.353 --> 00:01:42.406
"Mew mew gaga" song.

00:01:42.406 --> 00:01:43.239
Right. "Mew mew gaga" song.

00:01:44.347 --> 00:01:46.376
"Gaga." Yeah. That's very important.

00:01:46.376 --> 00:01:47.840
You did it very well, by the way.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.400
Not a lot of people can do it.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.930
And who have you been
trying to get to do it?

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.160
I heard you try to, you went on,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.280
was it Ridges and?

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.260
Yeah, Ridges. He was a champ.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:00.020
He did it and he did it very well.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.560
And the first thing...

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.160
Did he know what he was
doing? How did this play?

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.200
Yeah. No we went through
the entire song actually.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.080
He what he knew, he knows the
chorus, the bridge everything.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.150
Right.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.819
I'd love to see Ridges scream
"mew mew gaga mew mew."

00:02:12.819 --> 00:02:14.740
And he's such a high energy guy.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:18.260
Right. He also, he can add
a little "gika" or "jenga"

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.826
with the two which is
nice. Just a little flavor.

00:02:20.826 --> 00:02:22.300
You know when they "mew
mew gaga" too much.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:23.780
I prefer not to go that
way. That's not me.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:25.910
That's more Ridges. I don't do that.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.240
I'm a strict "mew mew gaga" guy myself.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.000
Okay. Well, whatever
makes you comfortable.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:30.993
It's your show. You know what I mean?

00:02:30.993 --> 00:02:32.500
I don't wanna push anything on it.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:33.490
After The Wendy's thing, I don't know

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:34.645
if it's mine anymore. It might be..

00:02:34.645 --> 00:02:35.478
(crowd laughs)

00:02:35.478 --> 00:02:38.990
It might be gone now. Now
you had to, have not had to

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.880
this I think has to be a thrill is present

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.120
at the Country Music Awards

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:46.510
which, you know, I mean,
you're playing Johnny Cash

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.040
in the film and then
you're asked to present

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.390
at the Country Music Awards. How was that?

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.040
Did you enjoy it?

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:52.510
Well, did you know how many people sat

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:53.480
at Madison square garden?

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.496
Do you know how many people are there?

00:02:55.496 --> 00:02:56.520
(crosstalk)

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.596
Slightly more than what we
have in this room right now

00:02:57.596 --> 00:02:58.580
(crosstalk)

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.670
A little bit more. Two and
a half million people. Wow.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.136
And not really

00:03:03.136 --> 00:03:05.264
(crowd laughs)

00:03:05.264 --> 00:03:07.990
But there was a lot.
That's what it feels like.

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:11.550
And I walked out there and I
just, I didn't know what to do.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.520
And I saw Kris Kristofferson right away.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:14.890
And he gave me a thumbs up

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:16.030
And I was like, "Well that's good."

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:16.900
I don't know what that meant.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.170
I don't know how he knows
that it's gonna be okay.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.003
But somehow...

00:03:20.003 --> 00:03:21.220
There's something very calming

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:22.830
about Kris Kristofferson.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:23.663
Yeah. No, it's true.

00:03:23.663 --> 00:03:24.770
I found that throughout my life,

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.720
whenever I'm in a moment
of crisis, I look around

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.626
and Kris Kristofferson appears.

00:03:28.626 --> 00:03:29.826
(crowd laughs)

00:03:29.826 --> 00:03:32.307
So he gives me a thumbs
up. He's like an angel

00:03:33.806 --> 00:03:36.326
He is. No, so that was good.

00:03:36.326 --> 00:03:39.900
And then I had to, you
know, we're presenting.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.340
And so we're. They go
through a list of names.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:43.550
It's on a teleprompter.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.290
And I was a little nervous

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.470
but I got all of those names right.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:50.140
But Reese who I've worked
with for like a year,

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:51.270
I got her name wrong.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.840
I called her Reeth on live television

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.748
and she kicked me off of stage.

00:03:57.748 --> 00:03:59.280
Yeah. She can be vicious
I'm told. Yeah. She's tough.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.810
No she's a sweetheart.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:04.460
Well, you guys, I mean she
plays your love interest,

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.610
June Carter Cash in this film.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:08.250
And I wanna talk about the movie now.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:12.900
First of all this great honor
you got to meet Johnny Cash,

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.200
even before you made this film.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.040
Before you knew that you were
gonna be making this film.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:17.873
Is that right?

00:04:17.873 --> 00:04:18.706
Yeah.

00:04:18.706 --> 00:04:19.539
How did that come about?

00:04:19.539 --> 00:04:21.870
Well, I was invited over to dinner

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:25.870
at his friend's house and went over

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.130
and it was an amazing experience.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:30.899
I can't tell you what that's
like no words can describe

00:04:30.899 --> 00:04:34.760
what it was there. John and
June were just beautiful people.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.120
And they sang this song together

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.640
and looked at each other's eyes

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.110
and it was just beautiful spiritual.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:42.100
And then I had to leave

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:45.210
and John stopped me as I was walking out.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.220
And he was a real fan of
this movie, "The Gladiator."

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:51.100
And he said, I can't do his voice anymore.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:55.279
But he said, "My favorite
part is when you said,

00:04:55.279 --> 00:04:57.560
"Your son squealed like a girl

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:59.080
when they nailed him to the cross

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:01.180
and you're a wife moaned like a horse.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.200
They ravaged her again
and again and again."

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.185
I love that part."

00:05:05.185 --> 00:05:07.768
(crowd laughs)

00:05:08.789 --> 00:05:10.289
We all remember that. Yeah.

00:05:10.289 --> 00:05:12.850
Yeah. I mean the most sadistic dialogue

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:14.170
ever committed to celluloid.

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:17.860
Right. That's what like
caught his attention.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:21.091
Well, he's an artist who went
to a dark place many times

00:05:21.091 --> 00:05:22.451
and he's fuscous.

00:05:22.451 --> 00:05:23.284
But he was amazed that that kind of him,

00:05:23.284 --> 00:05:26.330
just seeing him with with
June and this great love

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.270
and then that's the
dialogue that he quotes

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:29.720
and it's fascinating.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.430
You know, one of the
things you do in the movie

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:33.720
in addition to, you
know, playing the music

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.930
and singing it is that
you have in the movie,

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:38.930
his deep baritone. And
I was just thinking,

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:40.440
how did you do that?

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.430
I mean, you could put a gun to my head.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:45.390
I could never get my voice
lower than like a helium squeak.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.367
But you have, I'd be like,

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:49.530
"Hi everybody. I'm Johnny Cash."

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:53.750
But you, I almost got that
role and that cost me.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:57.490
But you really you changed your voice

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.290
and I'm wondering how do you do that?

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.620
With vocal lessons, doing
working with a vocal coach.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:06.940
And it's the most humiliating
process I've ever endured

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:09.560
because you do these
exercises, all these vowels.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.940
And you do like
"thethethethe," up and down.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:16.400
And it's so uncomfortable.
I can't tell you.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.330
And to transition as I hear this guy

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:20.560
going "funkdy bangdy dingidy dingidy" up.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.100
You know how do you go
from that to I shot a man

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.080
and really don't wanna
watch him die. It's strange.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.080
It's not getting you in the mood

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:28.765
to sing a Johnny Cash song.

00:06:28.765 --> 00:06:30.350
It was very, very difficult.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.730
Well, we have a clip here.
This is a great scene actually.

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:36.650
This is in the movie.

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:39.090
Johnny Cash has gone through
this terrible problems

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:41.190
with addiction. He's been gone for a while

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:42.490
and then he wants to come back

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:46.100
and he wants to appease this
idea to sing at Folsom prison.

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.600
His record label says,
"That's a stupid idea,

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.350
no one wants to see that,"
but he goes ahead and does it.

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:54.100
And I think it's one of
the biggest selling records

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.260
of all time when this came out.

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:56.750
It sounds great. It seems
like an interesting movie.

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:59.046
Yeah, thank you very much.

00:06:59.046 --> 00:06:59.879
I look forward to seeing it.

00:06:59.879 --> 00:07:02.334
I like to set up all the
clips myself. I just enjoy it.

00:07:02.334 --> 00:07:04.740
(crowd laughs)

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:07.840
Anyway here's a little thing
I did called Walk the Line

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:09.880
but take a look at this clip.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:12.310
My hats off to you now.

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:14.710
Cause I ain't never had
to drink this yellow water

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:15.804
you got here at Folsom.

00:07:15.804 --> 00:07:19.137
(crowd applauds loudly)

00:07:23.004 --> 00:07:25.754
(glass shatters)

00:07:26.796 --> 00:07:29.499
This song's for your warden.

00:07:29.499 --> 00:07:33.150
♪ early that morning while
I'm making the rounds, ♪

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:37.074
♪ I took this shout of cocaine
then I shot my woman down ♪

00:07:37.074 --> 00:07:41.099
♪ I went riding home then I went to bed. ♪

00:07:41.099 --> 00:07:45.633
♪ I stuck that lovin' 44 beneath my head ♪

00:07:45.633 --> 00:07:49.183
♪ got up next morning
and I grabbed that gun. ♪

00:07:49.183 --> 00:07:53.299
♪ took a shot of cocaine and away I run ♪

00:07:53.299 --> 00:07:56.707
♪ made a good run but I run too slow ♪

00:07:56.707 --> 00:08:01.488
♪ they overtook me down
in Juarez, Mexico ♪

00:08:01.488 --> 00:08:03.696
(crowd applauds)

00:08:03.696 --> 00:08:07.475
I've heard from, you know
I think a lot of people

00:08:07.475 --> 00:08:12.100
and I thought I was very
familiar with Johnny Cash's work

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.310
and a fan of his. I saw
this movie and I went home

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:15.680
that night and I stayed
on the internet all night

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.510
like getting more Johnny Cash songs

00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:18.810
and trying to look at rare footage.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:22.400
This movie makes you wanna
like revisit everything he did.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:23.800
Which is really cool.

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:26.920
Walk the Line opens
tomorrow.Go see this movie.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:27.840
It's fantastic

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.331
It's been an honor to
have you. Thank so much

00:08:30.331 --> 00:08:31.498
(crosstalk)

00:08:31.498 --> 00:08:34.248
(crowd applauds)

